gest loss of the early game, 28.1 points, taking Black's lead from +15.8 to -12.2. The engine wanted E2. GPT-6.1 Sol took E2 itself next move, losing only 0.2, and went from behind to comfortably ahead."}, {"turn": 42, "text": "White's A2 lost 43.6 points and flipped Black's lead from -19.8 to +23.9. The engine's choice was D4. The 20k then replied B7, losing 40.0 and handing the lead straight back at -16.1."}, {"turn": 50, "text": "The same story, even bigger: White's D9 lost 51.4 points, moving Black's lead from -27.1 to +24.3, and the engine still wanted D4. The 20k's B6 at move 51 lost 40.0 and gave it all back, ending at -15.7. This was the worst move of the game and its worst answer."}, {"turn": 54, "text": "GPT-6.1 Sol finally played D4, the point the engine had named in so many positions, and lost -0.1 doing it. With Black's lead at -28.5, the game was over, and the rest was filling in the edges."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "Everything it ran was bookkeeping: the inspect function in game-194-inspection.js parses the current board into groups, liberties and rooms, and game-194-verifier.js replays the actual game history to check legality and captures. Plus saving raw responses, SGF and notes via apply_patch and plain play calls. Nothing looped over candidate moves or looked ahead, so the moves were chosen manually.", "stop": false}}